# Source for q/ae-coronary-devices.js: Coronary & wall motion + Transplant & devices.
C="Coronary & wall motion"; T="Transplant & devices"
Q=[
# --- Segment model and scoring ---
(C,"The ASE-recommended LV segment model for wall motion and perfusion has how many segments?","17",["16","12","20"],"Six basal, six mid, four apical and the apical cap. The 16-segment model leaves out the apex cap.","F"),
(C,"In the 17-segment model, the apical level has how many segments, not counting the apical cap?","4",["6","3","5"],"Apical anterior, septal, inferior and lateral.","F"),
(C,"In wall motion scoring, a score of 1 means:","Normal or hyperkinetic",["Hypokinetic","Akinetic","Dyskinetic"],"2 = hypokinetic, 3 = akinetic, 4 = dyskinetic (aneurysmal in some schemes).","F"),
(C,"A wall motion score index (WMSI) of 1.0 means:","All scored segments are normal",["All segments are akinetic","Half the segments are abnormal","The study is not interpretable"],"WMSI = sum of segment scores ÷ number of segments scored. Higher values mean more abnormal wall motion.","F"),
(C,"Six segments score 2 and eleven score 1. The WMSI is about:","1.35",["1.0","2.0","1.65"],"(6 × 2 + 11 × 1) ÷ 17 = 23 ÷ 17 ≈ 1.35.","C"),
(C,"Akinesis is best defined as:","Little or no systolic thickening (under about 10%) and no inward motion",["Reduced but present thickening","Outward systolic motion","Excessive thickening"],"Always judge thickening, not just motion, because tethered segments can be pulled inward.","F"),
(C,"Why should wall thickening be judged rather than endocardial motion alone?","Translation and tethering can move a scarred segment even though it does not thicken",["Motion is easier to measure","Thickening never changes in ischemia","Motion is not affected by ischemia"],"Whole-heart movement and neighboring segments can make a dead segment appear to move.","F"),
(C,"Which LV segments are usually supplied by the LAD?","Anterior, anteroseptal and most apical segments",["Inferior and inferoseptal","Inferolateral only","Basal anterolateral only"],"A large 'wrap-around' LAD may also supply the apical inferior wall.","F"),
(C,"Which segments are usually supplied by the RCA in a right-dominant system?","Basal and mid inferior and inferoseptal walls, plus the RV",["Anterior wall","Apical cap only","Anterolateral wall"],"Proximal RCA occlusion can also cause RV infarction.","F"),
(C,"Coronary 'dominance' is defined by which artery gives the:","Posterior descending artery",["Left main","First diagonal","Conus branch"],"About 70–85% of people are right dominant.","F"),
(C,"Which territory can be variable and is shared between the LAD, RCA and LCx depending on anatomy?","The apex and inferolateral segments",["The basal anterior wall","The basal anteroseptum","The RV outflow tract"],"Segment-to-artery assignment is a guide, not a rule.","C"),
(C,"A wall motion abnormality limited to the basal and mid inferolateral walls is most typical of disease in the:","Left circumflex artery",["LAD","Left main only","Conus artery"],"The LCx supplies the lateral and inferolateral walls.","F"),
# --- Ischemia physiology ---
(C,"In the ischemic cascade, which happens first?","Perfusion abnormality",["Chest pain","ECG changes","Wall motion abnormality"],"Order: perfusion, diastolic then systolic dysfunction, ECG changes, then angina.","F"),
(C,"Myocardial stunning is:","Temporary dysfunction after ischemia is relieved, which recovers in days to weeks",["Permanent scar","Chronic dysfunction from ongoing low flow","Normal myocardium"],"Blood flow is restored but contraction lags behind.","F"),
(C,"Hibernating myocardium is:","Chronically underperfused, poorly contracting but viable muscle that can improve after revascularization",["Dead scar","Stunned muscle after reperfusion","Hypertrophied normal muscle"],"Detecting it changes management because revascularization can help.","F"),
(C,"On low-dose then high-dose dobutamine, a segment that improves first and then worsens (biphasic response) suggests:","Viable but ischemic myocardium",["Scar","Normal myocardium","Pericardial disease"],"This response predicts recovery after revascularization best.","C"),
(C,"A thin (under about 6 mm in diastole), bright, akinetic segment most likely represents:","Transmural scar",["Hibernating muscle","Stunned muscle","Normal muscle"],"Scarred segments rarely recover after revascularization.","F"),
(C,"Global longitudinal strain (GLS) in a healthy adult is typically about:","−20% (more negative than about −18%)",["+20%","−5%","−40%"],"Negative strain means shortening. Less negative values mean worse function.","F"),
(C,"In a 'bull's-eye' strain plot, a territorial area of reduced strain is useful because it:","Can show regional dysfunction matching a coronary territory",["Measures the ejection fraction directly","Removes the need for good images","Measures valve gradients"],"Values depend on vendor and image quality.","F"),
(C,"Post-systolic shortening (segments still shortening after aortic valve closure) is a sign of:","Ischemia",["Normal function","Aortic stenosis only","Pericardial effusion"],"It can appear on strain or tissue Doppler.","C"),
(C,"During an acute MI, segments away from the infarct often appear:","Hyperkinetic (compensating)",["Akinetic","Dyskinetic","Thinned"],"Loss of compensatory hyperkinesis suggests multivessel disease.","C"),
# --- MI complications ---
(C,"A true LV aneurysm most often forms in the:","Apical and anterior walls after LAD infarction",["Basal inferolateral wall","RV outflow tract","Left atrium"],"True aneurysms have a wide neck and a wall made of thinned scar.","F"),
(C,"A neck-to-maximum-diameter ratio of less than about 0.5 suggests:","An LV pseudoaneurysm",["A true aneurysm","A normal LV","A diverticulum only"],"Pseudoaneurysms have a narrow neck and are lined by pericardium.","C"),
(C,"LV pseudoaneurysms most often follow infarction of the:","Inferior or inferolateral wall",["Apex","Anterior wall","Anteroseptum"],"They need urgent surgery because they tend to rupture.","C"),
(C,"Post-MI ventricular septal rupture after an inferior MI is usually located in the:","Basal inferior septum",["Apical septum","Membranous septum","Atrial septum"],"After anterior MI, ruptures are usually apical and simpler in shape.","C"),
(C,"Papillary muscle rupture most often involves the posteromedial muscle because it:","Usually has a single blood supply",["Has a dual blood supply","Is larger","Is not attached to chordae"],"The anterolateral muscle usually gets blood from both LAD and LCx branches.","F"),
(C,"Free wall rupture after MI usually presents with:","Sudden tamponade and collapse, often in the first week",["A slow murmur over months","Atrial fibrillation only","Mild chest pain only"],"Look for a new pericardial effusion with echogenic clot.","F"),
(C,"Chronic ischemic MR after an inferolateral MI usually has:","Posterior leaflet tethering with a posteriorly directed jet",["A prolapsing anterior leaflet","Rheumatic thickening","A central jet with normal leaflets"],"Displaced papillary muscles pull the leaflet down (restricted motion in systole).","C"),
(C,"Which finding raises the risk of LV thrombus after MI?","Large anterior-apical infarct with akinetic or aneurysmal apex",["Small inferior MI","Normal LV function","Mild MR"],"Slow flow in a non-contracting apex favors clot.","F"),
(C,"An apical thrombus is suspected but the apex is poorly seen. The best next step is:","Use contrast and a high-frequency, zoomed apical view",["Ignore it","Use a lower frequency only","Measure the LVOT"],"Contrast makes the thrombus appear as a filling defect.","F"),
(C,"What helps separate an apical thrombus from a false tendon or trabeculation?","Thrombus is a mass with edges distinct from the endocardium, sitting over an abnormal segment",["Thrombus always moves with the wall","Thrombus is found only over normal segments","Thrombus never protrudes"],"Thrombus almost never forms over normally contracting myocardium.","C"),
(C,"Spontaneous echo contrast ('smoke') in the LV apex suggests:","Very slow blood flow and a raised clot risk",["Normal flow","Contrast was injected","Valve stenosis only"],"It is often seen in severely reduced LV function.","F"),
(C,"An RV infarct is most likely with an occlusion of the:","Proximal RCA",["Distal LAD","First diagonal","Obtuse marginal"],"RV branches arise from the proximal RCA.","F"),
(C,"Echo signs of RV infarction include:","RV dilation, RV free wall hypokinesis and reduced TAPSE",["A small hyperkinetic RV","Thickened RV wall","Normal TAPSE with RV aneurysm"],"Patients may be very sensitive to low preload.","F"),
(C,"In LV failure after MI, a restrictive mitral filling pattern (E/A over 2, short deceleration time) means:","High LA pressure and a worse prognosis",["Normal filling","Impaired relaxation only","Low LA pressure"],"It often improves with treatment.","C"),
# --- Other coronary conditions ---
(C,"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy typically shows:","Apical ballooning with hyperkinetic basal segments, often after emotional or physical stress",["Inferior wall akinesis only","A thickened septum","Normal apex with akinetic base always"],"The abnormal area extends beyond one coronary territory, and function usually recovers.","F"),
(C,"Which complication can occur in Takotsubo because the base contracts very strongly?","Dynamic LVOT obstruction with SAM",["Aortic dissection","Mitral stenosis","Pericardial constriction"],"Inotropes can make it worse.","C"),
(C,"A wall motion abnormality that crosses coronary territories should raise suspicion of:","Non-ischemic causes such as Takotsubo or myocarditis",["A single-vessel MI","Normal variant","LBBB only"],"Correlate with angiography and clinical history.","F"),
(C,"Left bundle branch block causes which septal motion?","An early inward 'beak' or flash, then abnormal timing of septal motion",["No change","Septal hypertrophy","Dyskinesis of the lateral wall"],"Septal thickening is preserved unless there is infarction.","F"),
(C,"Coronary flow reserve can be measured on transthoracic echo by:","Comparing distal LAD diastolic velocity at rest and at peak stress",["Measuring LVOT VTI only","Measuring TAPSE","Measuring the IVC"],"A ratio under about 2 suggests reduced flow reserve.","C"),
(C,"Spontaneous coronary artery dissection (SCAD) most commonly affects:","Young to middle-aged women, including around pregnancy",["Elderly men with heavy calcification only","Infants","Marathon runners only"],"Echo shows a wall motion abnormality like other MIs.","C"),
(C,"A coronary artery fistula most often drains into the:","Right side of the heart (RV, RA or pulmonary artery)",["Left atrium only","Aorta","Pericardium"],"It may cause a dilated feeding coronary and continuous flow on color.","C"),
(C,"In ALCAPA, why are collateral vessels seen in the septum on color Doppler?","The RCA supplies the left coronary through collaterals, with flow reversing into the PA",["They are normal in all children","They show aortic regurgitation","They show a VSD"],"Flow in the left coronary moves backward into the pulmonary artery.","C"),
(C,"A coronary artery aneurysm in a child should make you think of:","Kawasaki disease",["Rheumatic fever","Marfan syndrome only","ALCAPA"],"Coronary Z-scores are used to grade dilation.","F"),
(C,"Cardiac troponin rise with a normal echo and normal coronaries could still be from:","Myocarditis, pulmonary embolism, or kidney disease, among others",["Only a large MI","Nothing else","Mild valve disease only"],"Echo findings are interpreted with the clinical picture.","F"),
(C,"In chronic ischemic heart disease with a dilated LV, the LV shape often becomes:","More spherical",["More elliptical","Smaller and thicker","Normal shape"],"Remodeling increases sphericity and worsens functional MR.","F"),
# --- Transplant ---
(T,"After a bicaval heart transplant, the atria usually look:","Close to normal size and shape",["Very large with a waist","Absent","Calcified"],"The biatrial method leaves a large 'snowman' atrium with a suture ridge.","F"),
(T,"Resting heart rate in a transplanted heart is usually:","Higher than normal (about 90–110 bpm)",["Lower than normal","Exactly the same as before","Irregular in all patients"],"The heart is denervated, so vagal slowing is lost.","F"),
(T,"Tricuspid regurgitation after heart transplant is common, often due to:","Damage from repeated endomyocardial biopsy",["Rheumatic disease","Endocarditis in all patients","Normal aging"],"A flail tricuspid leaflet can develop.","F"),
(T,"The gold standard for diagnosing acute rejection after heart transplant is:","Endomyocardial biopsy",["Transthoracic echo","Chest X-ray","ECG"],"Echo helps with screening and follow-up.","F"),
(T,"Cardiac allograft vasculopathy (transplant coronary disease) is:","A diffuse, accelerated narrowing of coronary arteries that is often silent",["A focal plaque only","Normal","Seen only in the first week"],"Patients may have no angina because the heart is denervated; dobutamine stress echo is used to screen.","F"),
(T,"Pericardial effusion in the early weeks after transplant is:","Common, and needs follow-up for size and tamponade",["Never seen","Always a sign of rejection","Always benign so it needs no follow-up"],"A new or growing effusion later can suggest rejection.","F"),
(T,"Mild RV dilation and dysfunction early after heart transplant is often from:","The donor RV facing raised pulmonary pressure in the recipient",["Normal aging","Pericardial constriction","Aortic stenosis"],"RV failure is a major cause of early graft failure.","C"),
# --- LVAD ---
(T,"Where is the inflow cannula of a typical LVAD placed?","At the LV apex, pointing toward the mitral valve",["In the ascending aorta","In the RA","In the pulmonary artery"],"The outflow graft goes to the ascending aorta.","F"),
(T,"With an LVAD at a good speed, the interventricular septum should be:","Midline, not bowing to either side",["Bowed far into the LV","Bowed far into the RV","Not visible"],"Leftward bowing means the LV is overemptied; rightward means it is under-unloaded.","F"),
(T,"An LVAD speed that is too high may cause:","A small LV, leftward septal shift and suction events",["A larger LV","Rightward septal shift","More aortic valve opening"],"Suction can cause arrhythmias and low flow.","F"),
(T,"A ramp study for an LVAD involves:","Changing pump speed step by step while measuring LV size, septal position, aortic valve opening and MR",["Measuring heart rate only","Exercise testing","Injecting contrast only"],"It helps set the best speed and check for pump thrombosis.","F"),
(T,"Why is some aortic valve opening preferred in a patient with an LVAD?","It reduces the risk of aortic root thrombus and valve fusion",["It raises the pump speed","It causes aortic stenosis","It is not important"],"Opening every one to three beats is often a target.","C"),
(T,"Which valve problem tends to develop over time with a continuous-flow LVAD?","Aortic regurgitation",["Mitral stenosis","Pulmonary stenosis","Tricuspid stenosis"],"AR in an LVAD is often continuous through systole and diastole, creating a blind loop.","F"),
(T,"Before LVAD implant, a PFO is important because:","After LV unloading, LA pressure drops and a right-to-left shunt can cause hypoxia",["It improves pump flow","It prevents aortic regurgitation","It is not important"],"PFOs are usually closed during surgery.","C"),
(T,"The most important cause of poor outcome right after LVAD implant is often:","Right ventricular failure",["Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Bradycardia"],"Assess RV size, TAPSE and TR before and after implant.","F"),
(T,"Inflow cannula velocity on color and PW Doppler in an LVAD is normally:","Low (often under about 1.5 m/s) and laminar",["Over 4 m/s","Absent","Reversed"],"High, turbulent velocities suggest obstruction or malposition.","C"),
# --- Other support devices ---
(T,"In an Impella device, the inlet should sit:","About 3.5 cm below the aortic annulus, inside the LV, free of the mitral apparatus",["In the LA","Above the aortic valve","In the RV"],"The outlet is above the aortic valve in the aorta.","C"),
(T,"In VA ECMO, which echo concern is especially important?","Raised LV afterload with poor aortic valve opening, LV distension and clot risk",["Low LV afterload","Aortic stenosis only","Pulmonary stenosis"],"Watch for a closed aortic valve and stasis in the LV and aortic root.","C"),
(T,"Which condition is a contraindication for an intra-aortic balloon pump?","Significant aortic regurgitation",["Cardiogenic shock","Mitral regurgitation from papillary rupture","Unstable angina"],"Diastolic inflation would increase regurgitant flow. Aortic dissection is also a contraindication.","F"),
# --- Leads, pacing, closure devices ---
(T,"A pacemaker or ICD lead in the right heart is seen on echo as:","A bright linear echo, often with reverberation, from the RA across the tricuspid valve into the RV",["A thin echo in the LV","A mass in the aorta","An echo in the pulmonary veins"],"Check for lead-related TR and vegetations.","F"),
(T,"In cardiac resynchronization therapy (CRT), the LV lead usually reaches the LV through the:","Coronary sinus to a lateral vein",["Mitral valve","Aortic valve","Atrial septum"],"CRT treats mechanical dyssynchrony in patients with wide QRS.","F"),
(T,"Right ventricular apical pacing usually causes which septal motion pattern?","An LBBB-like dyssynchronous pattern",["Normal timing","Septal hypertrophy","No septal motion change"],"Chronic RV pacing may lower LV function.","F"),
(T,"A leadless pacemaker is typically placed in the:","RV",["LV","LA","Aorta"],"It looks like a small bright capsule attached to the RV wall, often near the septum.","F"),
(T,"Before percutaneous left atrial appendage closure, TEE is used to:","Rule out LAA thrombus and size the appendage ostium",["Measure the RV","Close a VSD","Assess the aorta only"],"After implant, TEE checks for leaks and device-related thrombus.","F"),
(T,"Which ASD type can be closed with a percutaneous occluder?","Secundum ASD with adequate rims",["Primum ASD","Sinus venosus ASD","Coronary sinus ASD"],"A deficient rim, especially posterior or inferior, can prevent device closure.","F"),
(T,"After a transcatheter aortic valve replacement, a key echo check is for:","Paravalvular regurgitation",["Mitral stenosis","Pulmonary vein flow","A VSD"],"Paravalvular leaks are more common with TAVR than surgical valves.","F"),
(T,"After a transcatheter edge-to-edge mitral repair (clip), the mitral valve normally shows:","A double orifice on short-axis view",["A single, normal orifice","A complete closure","A prosthetic disc"],"Check the mean gradient to make sure the repair has not caused significant stenosis.","F"),
]
